 to go  build a company that's going after  something small  as something that's big and in some ways  it's actually easier to go build a  company that's going after a big thing  because it inspires people around you to  want to get involved right  and that's from the team that's from  advisors that's from investors  so the bigger the mission you go after  ironically enough the more people show  up to help you because it's actually  worthwhile pursuing  you know what i love about you is um  i've talked to a lot of vcs  and you feel more like an entrepreneur  than a vc if that makes sense it's like  absolutely i mean the way this started i  was sitting in the hot tub with my wife  and i was kind of complaining about how  broken vc was in this particular sector  yeah and she's like you're an  entrepreneur that's a problem fix the  problem build a product or a service  and so we did like i i think of us as  entrepreneurs who are building a product  and service  for founders and that product is prime  movers lab where  the least useful thing we provide is the  capital it's all the other supplementary  technical expertise uh portfolio support  um coaching  that makes us uh really uh not  not an investment shop but company  builders who happen to have pre-raised  some capital  so where do scientists or  or kind of entrepreneurs with the  science background where do they  struggle  when they're trying to raise capital  there's a lot of places  i think uh historic historically uh you  know  if you do a phd and become an expert in  neuroscience or something like that  you may be very good at communicating to  an academic audience  but you may not know how to tell a  business story and how to market  kind of what the commercial opportunity  is so that's one thing that we often  even even like i think these are simple  things that when you  frame what your mission is is to serve  founders it's really different than if  your mission is to invest in founders  those are  subtle subtle shifts but really  important and of course we end up  investing in founders  but in the diligence process we'll often  be interacting with a founder  and we'll be giving them feedback and  advice about how they can be telling  their story better  from the first pitch even if they  haven't committed to work with us we  haven't committed to work with them  so that they get that benefit as they're  pitching to multiple investors and i  think  uh that's one simple example uh there's  all the operational pieces right  you're not prepared necessarily uh if  you've built some  amazing breakthrough to uh to sell to  market  uh to hire and so we have kind of all  the resources you need across the  spectrum  to prepare to deal with each of those  challenges which frankly are  uh you know you don't need to reinvent  the wheel people have figured out how to  do those things  and you can take playbooks and copy  those and apply them to the particular  business that's being built how much of  a business  background do you think somebody has to  have like when they're pitching a video  someone's pitching you hey  you just raised 245 million dollars now  you got all these people  emailing you and say hey i want some of  that deakin give me some  uh what are you looking for  so yes the hardcore tech the  breakthrough science  what else are you looking for from the  founder and how much of it of a team and  complete package do they need to have  so we evaluate companies on four  criteria so the first criteria  is the founder and the founding team the  management team around them and so we're  looking for people who really are  relentless  and they're willing to take feedback  those are like the two most important  criteria i think in any successful  founder  because when you when you start a  company you're really going after this  like huge vision  and you need to be laser focused i'm  going to do whatever it takes to make  sure that this vision succeeds  but you need to be really flexible and  adaptable to what's the pathway to  getting to that success  because you're gonna learn from your  customers you're gonna learn from  investors you're gonna learn from your  team learn from partners  things that are often the exact details  about how to get to that  so that's that's the first piece the  second piece is  the technology of course there needs to  be real defensible intellectual property  for our particular investment thesis and  the way that we invest  third is the business and so uh you know  often founders will come to us  that are clearly in love with the  science of what they've done  and they happen to be building a  business that tends not to be the right  uh exact founder for us we want folks  who really want to make an impact on the  world that are wanting to make a big  contribution and  they see the business as a vehicle to  scale their science out to serve  humanity and so there has to be a big  market there has to be a big pain point  and there's to be thoughtfulness about  the business they don't have to have all  the answers you're not gonna have all  the answers at the beginning  but there has to be an orientation  towards that and then of course we're  investing at a stage where we're putting  in  two to five million dollars so this  would be kind of an early stage company  that's just at the beginning of that  commercialization pathway  for the first one of taking feedback how  do  how do you test for that is that do you  have do you have  tests or hoops that you throw people  through or is it just a  vibe that you get when you first meet  them yeah i think you get a vibe at  first but we  we give people feedback during the  process  and we see how they incorporate it and  you know there's varying levels of  reaction to that  so if somebody's got like a very strong  sense of ego  uh they might be quite resistant and  that that's quite obvious um  kind of the the best founders tend to oh  uh say something like oh thank you  that's like really useful information  and  they don't like i'm not saying uh i i'm  not arrogant enough to think that we  have the answers or that we we know the  right way to do things  i think that we can be a useful partner  in discussing things and so  they may come back and reflect on that  and say actually this piece i think  really applies this piece doesn't apply  so much  and we love that because we on the other  end of the spectrum you can have people  who are like  uh i'm just gonna do whatever you tell  me to do and that's not the right  founder mentality either they need to be  critical thinkers  in choosing feedback from many different  sources and identifying  this is the parts that i'm going to  implement how often do you have to  take them out of the ceo role yeah  we don't view there's definitely vc  firms that uh that's their orientation  that's what they're gonna do  yeah at the end of the day we're backing  founders with a great technology and  business opportunity  and if the founder uh isn't like this is  what we're trying to assess with the  feedback piece  if the founders not performing we will  come and deliver them feedback  and we will try and support them in  developing them and getting them to the  place where they can  tackle the challenge out of them if they  can't we will at a certain point say  hey we don't think that you're the right  person to be running this we think you  should bring in a partner or an ally  that helps you do this and complement  your skill set but if they're not  willing to do that we're not going to  force them to do that we're not in the  business of forcing uh ceos out  at that point we're going to focus our  attention resources somewhere else that  is accepting the feedback and is  accepting the guidance that we're  providing right  is that something that's already kind of  you're road mapping it when you're first  meeting them like before you even sign a  deal it's like hmm  okay is this the person who can actually  build a hundred million dollar company  plus at those first conversations we're  discussing with them  not just where do they want their  company to be and what kind of outcome  do they want to produce for the world  but what do they want to become and  often with scientific founders they're  very upfront like  i'm the cto i'm the chief scientist i  happen to be holding the ceo right now  role right now at some point we're going  to need to bring in a ceo  and that's that's fantastic we're very  comfortable with that as long as the  person is self-aware  reflective and introspective where  they're considering what's right for the  business you know there's a great phrase  uh do you want to be rich or you want to  be king and it's like a little crass but  i think it captures the point  where uh do you want to see your  technology make a meaningful difference  to hundreds of millions or billions of  people around the world  and at a certain point if it turns out  that you you're  holding that title and you're uh guiding  the ship  isn't the right answer are you willing  to give it up and that's that's  something we always ask founders  to understand what's the motivation is  it about them or is it about the mission  what do you think some of the biggest  mistakes these founders are making when  they're pitching  vcs or maybe maybe like a vc like you  might have a slightly different way of  operating  yeah i think i think one subtle easy  thing that people can do is  listen to the language that the other  party is using so if i'm  presenting to an investor that's a  potential investor for us the first  thing i always do is i ask them as many  questions as i possibly can  to the point where they they might even  get a little bit annoyed that i haven't  started talking i haven't started  communicating  but i'm hearing i'm hearing what matters  to them i'm hearing  what successes they've had when they  were investing in similar things what  failures they've had so i know which  things to avoid  uh focusing on and i'm getting as much  information from them before i start  telling my story as possible  because then i know how to communicate  my story in a way that's fitting for  them  and i might even assess in that period  like hey like this actually isn't even a  good person and be like really blunt  about that like  uh we're probably not a fit for you i  don't want to waste your time and  i think we often have this mindset when  we're trying to convince someone of  something  of presuming that we know that we how to  convince them up front  we generally do because we've told our  story a lot but our story has to be  customized  to that person's mindset to that  person's psychology to what matters to  them  i love your vibe i love the entrepreneur  mindset that you have  talking about you're stepping up  in front of the camera and doing  instagram lives like  vcs aren't doing this kind of stuff  right like how i know it's not about the  ego on you  and you know look at me deacon so so why  the  push to come in front of the camera and  spread the message the message  yeah i mean look it's definitely it's  definitely not about me uh if it was  just like a personal preference i  probably wouldn't do this i'm not like a  very uh public-facing person  uh as you can tell by the fact that i've  never done an instagram live um  that being said i think the mission that  our companies are on is so important for  the world  as i said we've got companies that are  feeding the planet they're providing  clean energy  that are taking us off to new worlds  that are ending the pandemic  and i think it's important to spread the  message about those companies  and i think uh i think of each of us as  vehicles to accomplish on purpose here  and so i'm a vehicle to help attract  great capital to those types of founders  that wouldn't necessarily be flowing  there so these important missions can  happen and  this is a there's a small piece in uh  doing that i appreciate you giving us  the opportunity to do that  um so that we can we can get the word  out there and we can get more phenomenal  founders funded that are working on  really mission critical problems  one of the things i talk to my audience  a lot about is making money has got to  be  in your top five list of priorities if  you're an entrepreneur but it can't be  number one  like there's the mission and the purpose  of number one and then money has to be  two to five but if it's too far down the  list  you don't have a business either because  you gotta make money i think a lot of  people have the perspective of vcs as  just being  cutthroat do whatever it takes to make  money  how do you balance the purpose  of the companies you're investing into  and the mission with the  need to make money to keep it growing  yeah  i think it's it's what you said it's  about order so  everyone wants to have a purpose  everyone wants to make money like those  are just universal things meeting basic  human needs that we have  the order matters a lot though if if you  over  prioritize making money you're going to  lose out i ultimately think on much  larger opportunities  because you're going to make short-term  oriented decisions rather than long-term  oriented decisions  if you focus on the big picture what's  the mission what's the state of the  world that i want to create  and it's an inspiring large uh state of  the world and there's a big gap there  you're going to produce plenty of plenty  of financial results too  you have to be thoughtful about those  things you have to be strategic and  tactical  but uh yeah i think i think this is  fundamentally one of the biggest  problems with vc  is vc has historically been far too  oriented towards  using founders as uh as their products  basically  rather than delivering a product to  founders to help them achieve their  mission  and uh as i said like our first fund is  right now on track to be one of the best  performing funds of 2018  less than three years after starting the  firm so i i think that  we have this notion that these are like  uh things we have to choose between we  don't have to choose between them  if we go after big missions and we  figure out how to serve a lot of people  um you know tony says this i don't know  if you said it when he was on with you  but  business is the ultimate spiritual game  you're trying to figure out how to add  more value than anybody else in the  marketplace  and if you do that for a large number of  people with a valuable product  you're going to produce great financial  results too you know as i look at you  i see the two differences in that one  you're  just the mindset and the mantra and the  and the caring for entrepreneurs and  and not just investing into them but  being their partner but then also kind  of like the deep  tech hardcore science that people are  afraid to jump into  um that might be a little harder to  emulate but the whole actually caring  about founders  is that something that you hope  permeates  vc culture and people start looking at  you as the model  well i don't need them to look at us as  the model but i uh i hope that it  permeates the culture and i think  to be fair there are other vcs out there  that take that approach in that mindset  as well  you know a good friend is uh joe  lonsdale who founded vc and they do they  do this really well in enterprise  software and bio attack and logistics  so they're they're definitely uh  founder-friendly firms out there they're  they're too far and a few between and i  think as we see a proliferation of  people  building startups we're going to see a  proliferation of this mindset and  expansion of those minds then it's  already starting to happen  for someone who's trying to raise like  if it's two to five million that they're  going to  get you know from your fund what's the  process like to apply is it just  go to your website there's an email send  us your your  executive summary is it you have to be  introduced like how are you actually  fielding most of your  inquiries yeah so we see about 3 000  deals a year  and uh definitely there's a weighting in  our preference to somebody who's made  the effort of getting an introduction  somehow through their network  versus uh just applying online and there  happens to be a higher quality  because look uh social proof matters um  social proof  is important to kind of validating  things and  it also it also shows it's not that hard  to get an intro to us you know like we  have big networks  um and uh if you didn't take the time to  do that it kind of  uh it can feel a little like lazy but  like look we look at everything  and we we do a quick screen where we're  evaluating founders on some of the  things we talked about before  because that's the easiest thing to  assess the business the deal  the technology those take more time but  the people i think you can form very  quick evaluations of what what's driving  people what's important to people  and it doesn't take a lot you just have  to ask a simple question what's  important to you  and just for people who maybe haven't  done a deal before if somebody's raising  the 2 to five million from you guys what  kind of percentage roughly are they  looking at giving up  it really depends on the stage of the  company and what they've accomplished so  far from a technical milestones  and from a commercialization perspective  but at a seed stage like that you're  generally selling between 10 and 25  of the company depending on where you're  at yeah  that's actually pretty good  yeah look at that we we want to be  aligned with our ceos and we want to  make sure they have tons of ownership  because they're going to be driving the  bus to success  and are people usually already having  angels when they come to you are you  trying to  just be a super angel almost coming in  at a much higher  valuation again it we've done some ve